george floyd's death sparked protest  around the world but 10 months on the  anger  has turned to apprehension will change  and justice come  as the criminal trial begins the civil  lawsuit concludes  with a record payout from the city of  minneapolis  to the floyd family the settlement  is not just historic because  of the 27 million dollars  paid out but for the impact  on social justice policy reforms  and police reforms the next chapter in  their fight for justice  is here former officer derek shoven is  on trial for murder  in the fortified courthouse but there's  a deep mistrust among  some not just for police but for the  system  already critical the jury selection is  too white  and concerned a payout isn't justice  without a conviction  so it really is a significant settlement  and it's a  significant indictment of the way that  police have treated african-american  people in this country for a very long  time  so i you know for that standpoint it's  significant  but um beyond that we really do have to  have  a conviction in the case of derek  chauvin we really need that because  otherwise  the only people who will have had a  consequence  for this killing will be george floyd  himself and the taxpayers  how confident are you that you will get  that conviction  frankly i'm concerned exactly a year on  the family of brianna taylor  still don't feel they have justice shot  dead  in a flawed police raid of her home in  louisville  her family received a payout but no  accountability for her killing  their deaths may have been a reckoning  for racial inequity  but for many only a conviction will feel  like  change sally lockwood sky news new york
 

